WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:03.228
Paper stalls unfold into a colourful
market.

00:00:03.228 --> 00:00:07.278
Buyers arrive with private spending
limits, while sellers bring goods that

00:00:07.278 --> 00:00:09.856
cost something to produce for each unit.

00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:19.847
A buyer pays less than the most they would
be willing to pay.

00:00:19.847 --> 00:00:24.043
The remaining gap glows above the price
tag, showing a benefit beyond the

00:00:24.043 --> 00:00:24.689
payment itself.

00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:35.562
Across the stall, the same price exceeds
the cost of producing an early unit.

00:00:35.562 --> 00:00:39.772
A second gap appears, this time on the
seller side of trade.

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:49.084
Many small unit gaps gather into two
shaded areas.

00:00:49.084 --> 00:00:54.426
One sits above the price line, while the
other sits below it and above

00:00:54.426 --> 00:00:55.189
marginal cost.

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:03.596
A fixed-cost receipt slides beneath the
seller area.

00:01:03.596 --> 00:01:09.119
It reminds us that producer surplus has
not yet paid every bill, so profit can

00:01:09.119 --> 00:01:09.856
be smaller.

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:19.022
The market pauses where the two curves
meet.

00:01:19.022 --> 00:01:24.924
How much extra value goes to buyers, how
much goes to sellers, and which

00:01:24.924 --> 00:01:26.189
amount represents profit?
